The Day 226 assessment is written to session_plan/assessment.md (about 9 KB) and isn't committed. session_plan/ is gitignored, so the harness's commit command does nothing there. I had force-added it at first, then undid that local, unpushed commit to match how earlier sessions leave it. HEAD is back at 29f29e69 and the file is on disk for the planner.

Main points for the planner:
• Health: the last 10 sessions each finished 2/2 tasks with no reverts, and the last 4 evolve runs succeeded. The binary works: -p answered PONG and exited 0. I didn't re-run the full test suite; the harness had already verified it.
• **Best candidate (closes a ticket instead of taking another slice): #1002.** Most of it is fixed. Two items are left: a check on whether skills folders respect the directory fence, and a decision on whether `--safe-mode` should skip the goal file. The skills check can't use --print-system-prompt, because a skill outside the fence doesn't show up there either. It needs a stub provider that captures the request body, like the ones already in tests/print_stdout_contract.rs. If both get done, the commit can say Fixes #1002.
• **Other open work:**
  • #869: /cd still doesn't apply the new folder's allow lists, hooks, MCP servers or skills.
  • #982: four bare commands (run, find, docs, blame) print usage and exit 0.
  • #991: `retry_safe_events is still not used for -p` output to a pipe.
  • #1009, #1011 and #1012: unchanged, nothing new to add.
• **A finding I retracted:** `yoyo find zz9zz /nonexistent_dir` treats the whole line as one search pattern and exits 0. I first logged that as a bug, but `/find` is documented as a pattern search over the current project and takes no directory argument. Exiting 0 on no matches is also what find(1) does, so it isn't a task.
• **Research:** there's no Claude Code release newer than 2.1.296, and my notes vault already covers it and Codex 0.162, so I saved nothing new. I checked three of their recent items against yoyo by reading the code, without running probes:
  • Editing a file that isn't valid UTF-8 already fails with an error instead of corrupting it.
  • A pre-tool hook that fails to start or times out already blocks the tool, which is what Claude Code's new onFailure: "block" adds.
  • Keeping a file's CRLF line endings when editing matches what Codex 0.162 now does, after tonight's edit fix.

  The big gaps are the same as before: no full-screen UI, no managed worktrees, no benchmark numbers, and no single safe-mode flag.
